The onboarding domain, the fountain's cost, and how bottles read and write

Captures a domain of concerns that has now been worked out from scratch more than once. **Nothing
here is decided** — the value is that the re-deriving stops.

## The onboarding surface has been three different things

Described as the bottles', then as the library's, now split. Each move was reasonable and none was
written down, which is the whole reason for `ONBOARDING.md`.

**What is settled.** The library onboards people generally. A bottle found in the wild, carrying a
control code, onboards you with the bottle.

**Why the library cannot be the only door.** It may later be **constituency-bounded** — geoJSON. A
constituency-bounded onboarder is the wrong and only door for exactly the person who most needs one:
a stranger holding an artifact is in no constituency by definition.

**Why the bottle gets the job.** It is the free transit object — it moves and nothing else does,
which makes it the thing a stranger actually encounters. It is an onboarding magnet **by being
portable, not by being suited to governing anything.**

## The asymmetry, which is the part worth keeping

> **A bottle can put a control code in. It has no control after that.** Authority at minting time,
> never at runtime.

Three consequences, written down because a design will otherwise assume each one away:

1. **A code cannot be revoked by what minted it.** Scoping and expiry have to be decided at mint and
   carried inside it.
2. **The bottle cannot know who used it** — and not as a privacy preference. `AGENTS.md` already
   forbids the network: a bottle that arrived optically was authenticated socially and by nothing
   else. **A control code has to be self-sufficient offline or it is a login, not a control code.**
3. **So a code is an assertion, not a permission check.** It says the holder may be admitted, to
   whoever is later in a position to admit them. It cannot itself admit anybody.

That third one is where the seam still is: `OPEN.md` §7 asks what a code grants, who honours it, and
whether a stranger onboarded by bottle gets anything durable or simply gets to read what they hold.
The last is much smaller and might be the whole requirement.

## The vanity QR is not free, and the cost is computable

A vanity QR laid over a rendering destroys modules. Past what basic redundancy absorbs, the
compensation is **lengthening the fountain**.

> Every pixel of vanity is paid for in fountain length, which is paid for in frames, which is paid
> for in how long a person has to hold a phone steady.

What needs deciding is who owns the budget — a parameter with a cost the encoder reports, or a fixed
allowance a design may spend within. Filed as `OPEN.md` §5, because this is exactly the kind of trade
that gets settled by accident.

Derived while writing it: **it applies to the camera-read renderings only.** Vanity on the `stored`
rendering would be decoration nothing ever looks at — it is a QR for ants, read by a program that
already holds the file.

## Read and write, named and not designed

`OPEN.md` §6. Reading is closest to settled — the clean room says what a viewer may not do, and the
library asked for a player with no build step; neither says what the surface *is*. Writing is barely
started, and the phone case is the sharp one, because the README already promises a bottle is
"openable and editable on a phone."

## Two corrections to the stub

- **Co-mounted, not downstream.** "The library is a consumer" reads as a dependency. It is not: the
  library consumes bottles **by being stacked with them on a node** — civic, station, or a pure
  communications node. They meet on a node and neither is upstream. A dependency reading would put
  the boundary in the wrong place.
- **`AGENTS.md` gains the minting-time rule** as #6, since it is the one a design is most likely to
  break by reaching for a revocation path that cannot exist.

## Still not seated, and now for a stated reason

`OPEN.md` §3 records it: you are working this repository directly alongside `library.anecdote.channel`
until its obligations are discovered, and the concerns above do not yet group into constituencies
anyone could speak for. **A seat drafted now would be guessing one.** No `advocate.yml`, so the
runner names it and skips it.
